# What `docker build` must NOT upload into the build context.
#
# Two reasons things appear here.
#
# Correctness: node_modules is built for the host platform. `bcrypt` is a
# native addon, so a Windows or macOS copy cannot run on Linux — and if it were
# copied in, `npm ci` would not replace it and the image would fail at runtime
# with a confusing error. `dist` is excluded for the same reason in spirit: the
# image builds its own, and a stale host build silently shadowing it is exactly
# the bug that is hardest to spot.
#
# Security: this repository is public and the image may be pushed to a
# registry. Anything copied in is readable by anyone who pulls it, so real
# secrets must never be part of a layer. Configuration reaches a container
# through environment variables, never through a baked-in file.

# Dependencies and build output — the image produces its own.
#
# Every pattern is doubled with a `**/` form on purpose: Docker matches these
# against the whole path, so a bare `*.tsbuildinfo` catches only the repository
# root and misses apps/api/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo.
#
# .tsbuildinfo matters more than it looks. It is how `tsc -b` decides what is
# already compiled. Copying a host one in while excluding dist/ tells the
# compiler "this is built" when nothing is — producing an image with no
# JavaScript in it that fails only when a container starts.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
coverage
**/coverage

# Secrets. The template is kept: it documents the variables and holds no real
# values. Every other .env file is excluded, including apps/web/.env.local.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Version control and local tooling state.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.claude

# Not needed to build or run the application.
docs
stitch-design
README.md
CLAUDE.md
*.log
**/*.log
Dockerfile
**/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
